{
  "passage": "The Earth moves in two ways. It spins on its axis, an imaginary line through the North and South Poles, and one full spin takes about 24 hours. This movement is called rotation, and it causes day and night. At any moment, the half of the Earth facing the Sun has day, while the other half has night. The Earth also travels around the Sun in a path called its orbit. One full trip takes about 365 days and 6 hours, which is one year. This movement is called revolution. Every four years the extra hours add up to one whole day, so February then has 29 days, and that year is called a leap year. The Earth's axis is not upright. It is tilted at an angle of about 23.5 degrees, and it always points in the same direction in space. Because of this tilt, the Northern Hemisphere leans towards the Sun for part of the year and away from it for another part. When it leans towards the Sun, the Sun's rays fall on it more directly and the days are longer, so it is summer there. Many people believe that summer comes because the Earth moves closer to the Sun, but that is not the reason. In fact, the Earth is closest to the Sun in early January, when it is winter in India.",
